WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:06.240
The next keyword we will look at is used when&nbsp;
we are just looking to get a small sample&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:10.400
of what is in a table or tables that&nbsp;
have been joined. I have a query that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:15.920
shows people and their addresses. When I&nbsp;
execute this, we see that the query produces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:25.520
almost 19000 records. The query ran quickly here,&nbsp;
but if you notice that a query is doing so many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:31.840
things that it's running slowly, and you are just&nbsp;
testing a query, you can limit this to show a top&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:38.880
number of records. To make this happen, I will add&nbsp;
the keyword TOP after the word SELECT and then a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:46.480
number, 100 in this case. The top 100 records&nbsp;
will return as soon as I execute this statement.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:53.849
Should you decide you want this sorted a certain&nbsp;
way, as we saw elsewhere in this course, you would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.849 --> 00:01:01.440
need to add an ORDER BY clause to the end of the&nbsp;
query, as I will do here to sort this PostalCode,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:11.840
and then I will execute this again. Now, we&nbsp;
see the top 100 records sorted by postal code.

